WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:06.960
When the bus boycott come along in 1953, I was happy to help with that.

2
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.880
We met with Mr. Matthews and Reverend Jemison and others.

3
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:16.800
We had meetings, and I was available to get out and drive up and down the road, take people wherever they had to go.

4
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.160
It was like a daily job.

5
00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.080
It was a pleasure, you know?

6
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:23.780
My oldest daughter when she got out of high school she had a little summer job working over at Kean’s Laundry.

7
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.900
And this was after the boycott, she told me.

8
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.920
She got on the bus and she sat in the front.

9
00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:31.540
There wasn’t any seats, except some way to the back.

10
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:36.740
And the bus pulled over to the side, and after a while, about five cars of policemen drove up.

11
00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:38.340
They got on the bus and asked her, “Why are you sitting here?”

12
00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.420
She said, “Well, I can sit here.”

13
00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.560
They made her get in the back.

14
00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.680
That was our experience of the bus.
